   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "fig:16.3.2",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "critical-point",
   "prompt": "<p>For CO₂, $\\ka = 0.366$ m⁶ Pa mol⁻² and $\\kbvdw = 42.9\\times10^{-6}$ m³ mol⁻¹. Find (a) the critical temperature and (b) the critical pressure that the van der Waals equation predicts. ($\\kR = 8.314$ J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.)</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 304.0,
      "unit": "K",
      "hint": "Use $\\kR\\kTc = 8\\ka/27\\kbvdw$."
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 73.7,
      "unit": "bar",
      "hint": "Use $\\kPc = \\ka/27\\kbvdw^2$, then 1 bar = 10⁵ Pa."
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>(a) $\\kTc = 8\\ka/(27\\kR\\kbvdw) = 8(0.366)/[27(8.314)(42.9\\times10^{-6})] = 2.928/(9.630\\times10^{-3}) = 304.0$ K, the 31 °C of Figure 16.3.2. (b) $\\kPc = \\ka/(27\\kbvdw^2) = 0.366/[27(42.9\\times10^{-6})^2] = 7.37\\times10^{6}$ Pa $= 73.7$ bar.</p>"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a2",
   "source_id": "fig:16.3.3",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Analyze",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "critical-point",
   "prompt": "<p>The van der Waals isotherm of CO₂ at 10 °C dips to 46.1 bar and peaks at 58.7 bar; the critical temperature is 31 °C. How many real roots does the cubic in $\\kVbar$ have (a) at 10 °C and 30 bar, (b) at 10 °C and 50 bar, (c) at 10 °C and 70 bar, and (d) at 50 °C and 70 bar?</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 1,
      "tol": 0.01
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 3,
      "tol": 0.01
     },
     {
      "part": "c",
      "value": 1,
      "tol": 0.01
     },
     {
      "part": "d",
      "value": 1,
      "tol": 0.01
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>An isobar cuts a subcritical isotherm three times only between the loop's dip and peak. (a) 30 bar is below the dip, so the isobar cuts once, on the vapor side ($\\kVbar = 0.653$ L/mol); the other two roots are a complex pair. (b) 50 bar lies between 46.1 and 58.7 bar: three real roots, 0.0873, 0.116 and 0.311 L/mol (liquid, meaningless, vapor). (c) 70 bar is above the peak: one real root, the liquid at 0.0778 L/mol. (d) 50 °C is above the critical temperature, so every isobar cuts the isotherm once: one real root, 0.257 L/mol.</p>"
   }
  }
